module bindings.csharp.secretVerificationGate;

// A credential release that answers with a secret -- an API key, an access
// token, a client secret -- owes the caller one proof before it hands the
// credential over: verification of a secret only the legitimate owner holds.
// In C# that verification is an awaited call, and the guard idiom is
// `if (<gate> && !await VerifySecret(...)) reject; else release;`. Placing
// the verification as the second operand of the conjunction makes it
// skip-able: `&&` evaluates left to right, so any input that falsifies the
// gate operand -- a request field naming a key type, a header, a configured
// mode -- never reaches the verification at all, and the release proceeds on
// that input's authority alone. The whole class of "type/flag-gated
// re-authentication" bypasses reduces to this shape: the verification is not
// the whole guard, it is a conjunct somebody arranged to be optional.
//
// The anchors are the ecosystem's standard vocabulary, not one project's: a
// Verify/Check credential-verification call, a credential-named release
// (Api key, access token, client secret, secret key, credential) in the same
// function, and a guard conjunction whose compacted text negates its second
// operand (`&&!`). Making the verification the whole guard -- the fix -- or
// releasing no credential leaves the function unreported. Residual: the
// conjunction and the verification name are separate facts over the same
// function, so a function whose *unrelated* conjunction negates some other
// call while it verifies and releases elsewhere also reports; measured once
// per tree, the shape is rare enough to review rather than suppress.
binding secretVerificationGate {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function" and node.context.language == "csharp" and node.context.expr contains "&&!" and containsAny(node.context.call, ["VerifySecret", "VerifyPassword", "VerifyHashedPassword", "VerifyClientSecret", "VerifyApiKey", "VerifyCredentials", "CheckPassword", "CheckSecret"]) and containsAny(node.context.call, ["ApiKey", "ClientSecret", "SecretKey", "AccessToken", "Credential"])
  emit issue code.GatedSecretVerificationBypass at node
}
